1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:01,750
Léa: Hello everyone,

2
00:00:01,750 --> 00:00:04,240
and welcome to The Cozy French!

3
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:05,510
I'm Léa,

4
00:00:05,510 --> 00:00:07,840
and I'm so happy to be back with you

5
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:09,400
for a new moment together.

6
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:11,130
I hope you're doing well,

7
00:00:11,130 --> 00:00:12,920
wherever you are in the world.

8
00:00:12,920 --> 00:00:14,400
Mathieu: And I'm Mathieu!

9
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:15,960
Hello, dear friends!

10
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:17,750
Maybe you're listening to us

11
00:00:17,750 --> 00:00:19,160
while making your coffee,

12
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:20,440
on your way to work,

13
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:22,360
while taking a little walk,

14
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:25,080
or even still under your blanket.

15
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:26,320
Either way,

16
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:28,390
make yourselves comfortable.

17
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:30,760
We're delighted to be back with you.

18
00:00:30,760 --> 00:00:32,480
Léa: Before we start,

19
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:33,960
tell us in the comments

20
00:00:33,960 --> 00:00:36,240
which country you're listening from.

21
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:38,000
And above all, tell us:

22
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,350
are you more of a morning or an evening person?

23
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:45,240
Mathieu: Ah, Léa, that's a dangerous question.

24
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:47,030
Because I think

25
00:00:47,030 --> 00:00:49,600
you already know my answer.

26
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:52,040
Léa: Wait, let me guess.

27
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:54,270
You're a morning person...

28
00:00:54,270 --> 00:00:57,070
but only after your second coffee?

29
00:00:57,120 --> 00:01:00,540
Mathieu: Exactly! Before my coffee,

30
00:01:00,540 --> 00:01:02,640
I'm physically present,

31
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:05,200
but I can't guarantee that my brain

32
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:07,240
is working properly.

33
00:01:07,240 --> 00:01:10,800
Léa: Poor Mathieu! But actually, today,

34
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:11,790
we're going to talk

35
00:01:11,790 --> 00:01:14,000
about a topic that might interest you.

36
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:14,720
Mathieu: Oh really?

37
00:01:14,770 --> 00:01:17,470
How to survive before eight in the morning?

38
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:18,640
Léa: Not exactly!

39
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:20,670
Today, we're going to talk

40
00:01:20,670 --> 00:01:22,300
about our morning routine,

41
00:01:22,350 --> 00:01:24,660
and above all about those little habits

42
00:01:24,660 --> 00:01:26,190
that can help us

43
00:01:26,190 --> 00:01:29,190
start the day with more calm,

44
00:01:29,190 --> 00:01:31,520
energy and good mood.

45
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:33,410
Mathieu: Ah, a morning routine

46
00:01:33,410 --> 00:01:35,720
for a happier life.

47
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:37,040
I like that idea.

48
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:38,790
But reassure me, Léa,

49
00:01:38,790 --> 00:01:40,240
you're not going to ask me

50
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:42,080
to wake up at five o'clock,

51
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:43,830
run ten kilometres

52
00:01:43,830 --> 00:01:46,670
and take an ice-cold shower?

53
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:49,510
Léa: Absolutely not! Because for me,

54
00:01:49,510 --> 00:01:50,980
a good morning routine

55
00:01:50,980 --> 00:01:53,200
shouldn't be a competition.

56
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:55,090
It should simply help us

57
00:01:55,090 --> 00:01:56,440
feel better.

58
00:01:56,440 --> 00:01:58,920
Mathieu: Phew! I prefer that.

59
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:01,440
Because sometimes, on the internet,

60
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:04,760
you get the feeling that to have a successful day,

61
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:07,350
you need to have done twelve things

62
00:02:07,350 --> 00:02:09,760
before breakfast even starts.

63
00:02:09,760 --> 00:02:10,700
Léa: That's true.

64
00:02:10,700 --> 00:02:11,920
But today,

65
00:02:11,920 --> 00:02:14,550
we're going to take a different direction.

66
00:02:14,600 --> 00:02:17,220
We're going to talk about simple,

67
00:02:17,220 --> 00:02:19,200
realistic and pleasant things.

68
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:21,930
Habits that everyone can adapt

69
00:02:21,930 --> 00:02:23,120
to their own life.

70
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:25,680
Mathieu: And as always on The Cozy French,

71
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:26,950
we're going to learn

72
00:02:26,950 --> 00:02:28,920
new French expressions

73
00:02:28,920 --> 00:02:30,840
while chatting naturally.

74
00:02:30,840 --> 00:02:33,000
So, if you're learning French,

75
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:34,840
just listen calmly.

76
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:36,250
You don't need

77
00:02:36,250 --> 00:02:39,080
to understand every word right away.

78
00:02:39,080 --> 00:02:40,160
Léa: Exactly.

79
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:43,500
Let the sentences come naturally.

80
00:02:43,550 --> 00:02:46,440
And if you enjoy learning French with us,

81
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:49,520
remember to subscribe to The Cozy French.

82
00:02:49,520 --> 00:02:51,760
It makes us so happy

83
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:54,280
to see you again in every episode.

84
00:02:54,280 --> 00:02:57,000
Mathieu: Okay, Léa, I have a question to start with.

85
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,190
What does your ideal morning look like?

86
00:03:00,240 --> 00:03:02,800
Léa: My ideal morning? Hmm...

87
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:05,480
I wake up gently, without noise,

88
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:08,600
with a little light coming through the window.

89
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:10,480
I take time to stretch,

90
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:12,100
I make my tea,

91
00:03:12,150 --> 00:03:15,360
and I enjoy a few minutes of silence.

92
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:19,320
Mathieu: Wait... You just described an advert

93
00:03:19,320 --> 00:03:21,960
for a house in the countryside.

94
00:03:21,960 --> 00:03:24,080
Léa: Maybe! But I didn't say

95
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:26,280
that all my mornings looked like that.

96
00:03:26,280 --> 00:03:28,800
Mathieu: Ah! That's reassuring.

97
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:31,240
Léa: In reality, some mornings,

98
00:03:31,240 --> 00:03:32,760
I wake up late,

99
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:35,280
I look for my glasses for five minutes,

100
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:38,000
and I spill a bit of tea on the table.

101
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,780
Mathieu: Ah, now that's a real person!

102
00:03:40,830 --> 00:03:42,760
I was starting to worry.

103
00:03:42,760 --> 00:03:43,840
Léa: What about you, Mathieu?

104
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:45,510
How do your days begin?

105
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:47,660
Mathieu: Well, for a long time,

106
00:03:47,660 --> 00:03:49,550
my mornings always started

107
00:03:49,550 --> 00:03:50,960
the same way.

108
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:53,970
My alarm rang, I turned it off,

109
00:03:53,970 --> 00:03:56,040
then I looked at my phone.

110
00:03:56,040 --> 00:03:57,840
I read my messages,

111
00:03:57,840 --> 00:04:01,040
the news, a few notifications...

112
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:03,040
And without realising it,

113
00:04:03,040 --> 00:04:05,480
twenty minutes had disappeared.

114
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:07,910
Léa: Twenty minutes? Every morning?

115
00:04:07,960 --> 00:04:11,960
Mathieu: Sometimes thirty. But I'd rather not talk about it.

116
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:12,910
Léa: And then?

117
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:14,880
Mathieu: Then, I rushed.

118
00:04:14,880 --> 00:04:17,080
I took a quick shower,

119
00:04:17,080 --> 00:04:20,600
I made my breakfast at top speed,

120
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:22,350
and I left the house

121
00:04:22,350 --> 00:04:24,840
with this unpleasant feeling

122
00:04:24,840 --> 00:04:28,240
of already being behind on my day.

123
00:04:28,240 --> 00:04:31,030
Léa: Even when you weren't actually late?

124
00:04:31,080 --> 00:04:33,660
Mathieu: Exactly! It was strange.

125
00:04:33,710 --> 00:04:35,430
It was barely eight o'clock,

126
00:04:35,430 --> 00:04:37,490
and I already felt

127
00:04:37,490 --> 00:04:39,520
like I was racing against the clock.

128
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:41,470
Léa: I think a lot of people

129
00:04:41,470 --> 00:04:43,400
know that feeling.

130
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:46,540
We open our eyes, and straight away,

131
00:04:46,540 --> 00:04:48,400
our mind starts thinking

132
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:50,520
about everything we have to do.

133
00:04:50,520 --> 00:04:54,080
Mathieu: Answering messages, making lunch,

134
00:04:54,080 --> 00:04:57,040
going to work, paying a bill,

135
00:04:57,040 --> 00:04:58,760
finishing a project...

136
00:04:58,760 --> 00:05:02,270
Léa: And sometimes we forget something very simple:

137
00:05:02,320 --> 00:05:04,720
taking a few moments for ourselves.

138
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:05,660
Mathieu: That's true.

139
00:05:05,660 --> 00:05:07,460
But how can we change that

140
00:05:07,460 --> 00:05:09,880
without turning our morning

141
00:05:09,880 --> 00:05:11,660
into an endless list

142
00:05:11,660 --> 00:05:13,710
of new obligations?

143
00:05:13,760 --> 00:05:15,050
Léa: Actually, I think

144
00:05:15,050 --> 00:05:17,280
you have to start very small.

145
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:19,550
No need to change your whole life

146
00:05:19,550 --> 00:05:21,240
in a single day.

147
00:05:21,240 --> 00:05:23,550
Mathieu: Very small, I like that.

148
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:26,150
How much time are we talking about?

149
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:30,170
Léa: Maybe three minutes. Maybe five.

150
00:05:30,220 --> 00:05:32,320
What matters isn't how long it lasts.

151
00:05:32,320 --> 00:05:35,320
It's the way you begin.

152
00:05:35,320 --> 00:05:36,660
Mathieu: So, five minutes

153
00:05:36,660 --> 00:05:40,110
can really make a difference?

154
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:43,050
Léa: They won't solve all our problems,

155
00:05:43,050 --> 00:05:43,640
of course.

156
00:05:43,640 --> 00:05:45,580
But they can help us

157
00:05:45,580 --> 00:05:48,960
start the day a bit more mindfully.

158
00:05:48,960 --> 00:05:52,240
Mathieu: You know, I've noticed something.

159
00:05:52,240 --> 00:05:53,990
When I start my day

160
00:05:53,990 --> 00:05:55,280
in a rush,

161
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:57,450
I tend to keep that pace

162
00:05:57,450 --> 00:05:59,630
for several hours.

163
00:05:59,680 --> 00:06:01,960
Léa: Exactly. And the other way round,

164
00:06:01,960 --> 00:06:03,770
when we take a few moments

165
00:06:03,770 --> 00:06:05,680
to wake up calmly,

166
00:06:05,680 --> 00:06:08,490
we can sometimes feel more available

167
00:06:08,490 --> 00:06:09,640
for what comes next.

168
00:06:09,640 --> 00:06:12,760
Mathieu: So, first important question:

169
00:06:12,760 --> 00:06:15,510
what do we do during those five minutes?

170
00:06:15,560 --> 00:06:17,940
Léa: Well, we can simply sit down

171
00:06:17,940 --> 00:06:18,960
on the edge of the bed,

172
00:06:18,960 --> 00:06:22,000
open the curtains, look at the sky,

173
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:23,760
or drink a glass of water.

174
00:06:23,760 --> 00:06:25,430
Mathieu: Nothing more?

175
00:06:25,480 --> 00:06:27,060
Léa: Nothing more.

176
00:06:27,110 --> 00:06:28,270
There's no need

177
00:06:28,270 --> 00:06:30,680
to do anything impressive.

178
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:32,080
Mathieu: I really like that.

179
00:06:32,080 --> 00:06:33,440
Because sometimes,

180
00:06:33,440 --> 00:06:35,640
personal development advice

181
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:36,880
makes me feel

182
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:40,110
like I have to become a new person

183
00:06:40,110 --> 00:06:41,600
before eight in the morning.

184
00:06:41,600 --> 00:06:42,970
Léa: But you don't need

185
00:06:42,970 --> 00:06:45,320
to become a new person.

186
00:06:45,320 --> 00:06:46,590
You simply need

187
00:06:46,590 --> 00:06:49,000
to find a more pleasant way

188
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:50,600
of being yourself.

189
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:53,080
Mathieu: That's a lovely way to put it.

190
00:06:53,080 --> 00:06:56,630
Léa: And besides, everyone has their own rhythm.

191
00:06:56,680 --> 00:06:59,440
Some people love getting up very early.

192
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:02,280
Others need a bit more time.

193
00:07:02,280 --> 00:07:04,680
And that's perfectly normal.

194
00:07:04,680 --> 00:07:07,120
Mathieu: By the way, let's talk about the alarm clock.

195
00:07:07,120 --> 00:07:09,120
Because I think my relationship

196
00:07:09,120 --> 00:07:11,920
with my alarm clock isn't very healthy.

197
00:07:11,920 --> 00:07:14,310
Léa: Oh really? What did it do to you?

198
00:07:14,360 --> 00:07:16,720
Mathieu: It rings way too early.

199
00:07:16,720 --> 00:07:19,760
Léa: Mathieu, you're the one who chooses the time!

200
00:07:19,760 --> 00:07:22,500
Mathieu: Yes, but evening Mathieu

201
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:24,250
is much more optimistic

202
00:07:24,250 --> 00:07:25,990
than morning Mathieu.

203
00:07:26,040 --> 00:07:28,680
Léa: Ah, that I understand very well!

204
00:07:28,680 --> 00:07:30,560
Mathieu: In the evening, I tell myself:

205
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:34,040
tomorrow, I'll get up at half past six.

206
00:07:34,040 --> 00:07:36,880
I'll make a nice breakfast,

207
00:07:36,880 --> 00:07:40,280
read a few pages, do a bit of exercise...

208
00:07:40,280 --> 00:07:41,710
Léa: And the next day?

209
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:44,960
Mathieu: The next day, I negotiate with my alarm

210
00:07:44,960 --> 00:07:46,250
as if we were

211
00:07:46,250 --> 00:07:48,480
in a diplomatic meeting.

212
00:07:48,480 --> 00:07:50,710
Léa: Five more minutes?

213
00:07:50,760 --> 00:07:54,830
Mathieu: Exactly. And then five more minutes.

214
00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:56,760
And five more minutes.

215
00:07:56,760 --> 00:07:57,320
Léa: You know,

216
00:07:57,320 --> 00:08:00,280
that expression is very common in French:

217
00:08:00,280 --> 00:08:02,880
"Just five more little minutes."

218
00:08:02,880 --> 00:08:04,980
It's probably one of the sentences

219
00:08:04,980 --> 00:08:06,920
most often said in the morning.

220
00:08:06,920 --> 00:08:10,160
Mathieu: And one of the least respected too!

221
00:08:10,160 --> 00:08:12,040
Léa: But more seriously,

222
00:08:12,040 --> 00:08:13,480
I think it's important

223
00:08:13,480 --> 00:08:17,280
not to set yourself unrealistic goals.

224
00:08:17,280 --> 00:08:19,030
If you're very tired,

225
00:08:19,030 --> 00:08:21,410
forcing yourself to get up much earlier

226
00:08:21,410 --> 00:08:23,950
isn't necessarily a good solution.

227
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:27,300
Mathieu: Yes. Sometimes the best morning routine

228
00:08:27,300 --> 00:08:28,840
starts the night before,

229
00:08:28,840 --> 00:08:32,240
by going to bed at a reasonable time.

230
00:08:32,240 --> 00:08:33,190
Léa: Absolutely.

231
00:08:33,190 --> 00:08:35,320
Sleep is essential.

232
00:08:35,320 --> 00:08:37,070
And a pleasant routine

233
00:08:37,070 --> 00:08:39,000
can't always make up for

234
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:40,800
nights that are too short.

235
00:08:40,800 --> 00:08:43,230
Mathieu: You know what's helped me recently?

236
00:08:43,280 --> 00:08:44,380
I've started

237
00:08:44,380 --> 00:08:47,560
getting a few things ready the night before.

238
00:08:47,560 --> 00:08:48,710
Léa: Like what?

239
00:08:48,760 --> 00:08:52,040
Mathieu: My clothes, my bag, my keys...

240
00:08:52,040 --> 00:08:53,790
Little things.

241
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:55,950
Before, I often wasted time

242
00:08:55,950 --> 00:08:58,320
looking for what I needed.

243
00:08:58,320 --> 00:08:59,550
Léa: And now?

244
00:08:59,600 --> 00:09:01,490
Mathieu: Now, my mornings

245
00:09:01,490 --> 00:09:03,760
are a little less chaotic.

246
00:09:03,760 --> 00:09:05,990
Well, I still look for my glasses

247
00:09:05,990 --> 00:09:07,240
from time to time,

248
00:09:07,240 --> 00:09:09,640
but that's already progress.

249
00:09:09,640 --> 00:09:11,480
Léa: That's exactly it.

250
00:09:11,480 --> 00:09:14,320
We don't need to transform our whole life.

251
00:09:14,320 --> 00:09:15,770
Sometimes, all it takes

252
00:09:15,770 --> 00:09:19,000
is removing one small source of stress.

253
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,140
Mathieu: And every little improvement

254
00:09:21,140 --> 00:09:23,560
makes the morning a bit lighter.

255
00:09:23,610 --> 00:09:25,400
Léa: Speaking of lightness,

256
00:09:25,400 --> 00:09:28,240
I'd like to talk about a habit I love:

257
00:09:28,240 --> 00:09:30,560
opening the window in the morning.

258
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:32,030
Mathieu: Even in winter?

259
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:33,680
Léa: Even in winter!

260
00:09:33,680 --> 00:09:36,240
Well, not for an hour, obviously.

261
00:09:36,240 --> 00:09:39,440
But a few moments to feel the fresh air.

262
00:09:39,440 --> 00:09:41,190
Mathieu: Me, in winter,

263
00:09:41,190 --> 00:09:43,930
I prefer to admire the fresh air

264
00:09:43,930 --> 00:09:45,560
from inside.

265
00:09:45,560 --> 00:09:48,160
Léa: You're really difficult!

266
00:09:48,160 --> 00:09:53,150
Mathieu: I'm just very attached to my blanket.

267
00:09:53,200 --> 00:09:54,360
Léa: But you know,

268
00:09:54,360 --> 00:09:56,080
I really love that moment

269
00:09:56,080 --> 00:09:59,080
when the house starts to wake up.

270
00:09:59,080 --> 00:10:01,820
You hear a few sounds in the street,

271
00:10:01,820 --> 00:10:03,800
sometimes the birds,

272
00:10:03,800 --> 00:10:06,480
and the light changes gently.

273
00:10:06,480 --> 00:10:08,290
Mathieu: It's true that morning light

274
00:10:08,290 --> 00:10:11,520
has something special about it.

275
00:10:11,520 --> 00:10:13,990
Léa: Yes. And when we can enjoy

276
00:10:13,990 --> 00:10:15,640
natural light,

277
00:10:15,640 --> 00:10:17,880
it can help our body

278
00:10:17,880 --> 00:10:20,510
recognise the start of the day.

279
00:10:20,560 --> 00:10:23,290
Mathieu: In fact, for a little while now,

280
00:10:23,290 --> 00:10:24,470
I've been trying to go out for a walk

281
00:10:24,470 --> 00:10:26,160
for a few minutes in the morning

282
00:10:26,160 --> 00:10:28,240
when my schedule allows it.

283
00:10:28,240 --> 00:10:30,350
Léa: Oh, that's new!

284
00:10:30,400 --> 00:10:32,190
You hadn't told me.

285
00:10:32,240 --> 00:10:34,730
Mathieu: Yes. There's a little park

286
00:10:34,730 --> 00:10:36,280
near my place.

287
00:10:36,280 --> 00:10:39,920
Nothing extraordinary, but I like to stop by

288
00:10:39,920 --> 00:10:41,920
before I start working.

289
00:10:41,920 --> 00:10:44,590
Léa: And what do you do during your walk?

290
00:10:44,640 --> 00:10:46,120
Mathieu: Not much.

291
00:10:46,120 --> 00:10:49,260
I walk, I look at the trees,

292
00:10:49,310 --> 00:10:52,040
I sometimes pass the same people.

293
00:10:52,040 --> 00:10:53,790
There's even a man

294
00:10:53,790 --> 00:10:56,560
who walks a tiny dog

295
00:10:56,560 --> 00:10:59,960
with absolutely incredible energy.

296
00:10:59,960 --> 00:11:02,550
Léa: The man or the dog?

297
00:11:02,600 --> 00:11:04,350
Mathieu: Both, actually!

298
00:11:04,350 --> 00:11:06,850
But mostly the dog. It runs

299
00:11:06,850 --> 00:11:10,240
as if it had a very important mission.

300
00:11:10,240 --> 00:11:12,640
Léa: Maybe it has

301
00:11:12,640 --> 00:11:14,990
a morning routine too!

302
00:11:15,040 --> 00:11:17,250
Mathieu: A much sportier routine

303
00:11:17,250 --> 00:11:19,400
than mine, apparently.

304
00:11:19,400 --> 00:11:22,510
Léa: And how do you feel after that walk?

305
00:11:22,560 --> 00:11:25,280
Mathieu: Usually, a bit more awake.

306
00:11:25,280 --> 00:11:27,400
And above all, I feel

307
00:11:27,400 --> 00:11:30,000
like I've started my day for myself,

308
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,320
not only for my work.

309
00:11:32,320 --> 00:11:34,960
Léa: That's an interesting distinction.

310
00:11:34,960 --> 00:11:37,440
Because sometimes our mornings become

311
00:11:37,440 --> 00:11:41,070
nothing but preparation for our obligations.

312
00:11:41,120 --> 00:11:43,930
Mathieu: Yes. We get up to go to work,

313
00:11:43,930 --> 00:11:46,800
to drive the kids to school,

314
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:50,400
to get tasks done... And we forget

315
00:11:50,400 --> 00:11:53,760
that the morning is part of our life too.

316
00:11:53,760 --> 00:11:56,800
Léa: Exactly. It's not just

317
00:11:56,800 --> 00:11:58,420
a compulsory step

318
00:11:58,420 --> 00:12:00,480
before the important things.

319
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:03,880
Mathieu: The morning is already an important moment.

320
00:12:03,880 --> 00:12:06,760
Léa: And we can give it a little gentleness,

321
00:12:06,760 --> 00:12:09,060
even when we have little time.

322
00:12:09,110 --> 00:12:11,440
Mathieu: You've just given me an idea.

323
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:13,400
Maybe I could stop

324
00:12:13,400 --> 00:12:15,040
checking my messages

325
00:12:15,040 --> 00:12:16,760
during breakfast.

326
00:12:16,760 --> 00:12:20,680
Léa: Ah! Now we're getting to an interesting topic:

327
00:12:20,680 --> 00:12:22,320
the phone.

328
00:12:22,320 --> 00:12:24,920
Mathieu: I knew it would come back

329
00:12:24,920 --> 00:12:27,040
into the conversation.

330
00:12:27,040 --> 00:12:29,480
Léa: But careful, I'm not going to say

331
00:12:29,480 --> 00:12:30,930
that you should never

332
00:12:30,930 --> 00:12:33,560
use your phone in the morning.

333
00:12:33,560 --> 00:12:36,710
For lots of people, it's necessary.

334
00:12:36,760 --> 00:12:38,400
Mathieu: Yes. You might need

335
00:12:38,400 --> 00:12:41,320
to check the transport times,

336
00:12:41,320 --> 00:12:43,340
read an important message

337
00:12:43,340 --> 00:12:45,480
or contact your family.

338
00:12:45,480 --> 00:12:46,560
Léa: Absolutely.

339
00:12:46,560 --> 00:12:48,380
But there's a difference

340
00:12:48,380 --> 00:12:51,040
between using your phone

341
00:12:51,040 --> 00:12:52,640
for a specific reason

342
00:12:52,640 --> 00:12:55,220
and checking it automatically

343
00:12:55,220 --> 00:12:57,240
without even thinking about it.

344
00:12:57,240 --> 00:13:00,640
Mathieu: That's exactly what used to happen to me.

345
00:13:00,640 --> 00:13:03,860
I'd pick up my phone to check the time,

346
00:13:03,910 --> 00:13:05,550
and ten minutes later,

347
00:13:05,550 --> 00:13:07,080
I was watching a video

348
00:13:07,080 --> 00:13:08,930
that had absolutely nothing

349
00:13:08,930 --> 00:13:10,720
to do with my day.

350
00:13:10,720 --> 00:13:12,110
Léa: Like what?

351
00:13:12,160 --> 00:13:13,910
Mathieu: A video about how

352
00:13:13,910 --> 00:13:17,640
to build a wooden cabin in a forest.

353
00:13:17,640 --> 00:13:21,150
Léa: Mathieu! Are you planning to build a cabin?

354
00:13:21,200 --> 00:13:22,950
Mathieu: No, but now

355
00:13:22,950 --> 00:13:25,240
I almost know how to do it.

356
00:13:25,240 --> 00:13:28,430
Léa: That's the danger of little distractions!

357
00:13:28,480 --> 00:13:30,250
Mathieu: Yes. And the strangest thing

358
00:13:30,250 --> 00:13:33,840
is that after watching all those videos,

359
00:13:33,840 --> 00:13:35,630
I didn't even feel

360
00:13:35,630 --> 00:13:37,440
like I'd made good use of my time.

361
00:13:37,440 --> 00:13:39,950
Léa: Because your attention had already gone off

362
00:13:39,950 --> 00:13:41,880
in several directions.

363
00:13:41,880 --> 00:13:45,070
Mathieu: Exactly. So, I started

364
00:13:45,070 --> 00:13:47,640
trying something simple:

365
00:13:47,640 --> 00:13:50,840
waiting until I'd finished breakfast

366
00:13:50,840 --> 00:13:53,600
before looking at social media.

367
00:13:53,600 --> 00:13:55,270
Léa: And does it work?

368
00:13:55,320 --> 00:13:56,560
Mathieu: Not every day.

369
00:13:56,560 --> 00:13:57,940
But often enough

370
00:13:57,940 --> 00:14:00,520
for me to notice the difference.

371
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:02,240
Léa: That's already very good.

372
00:14:02,240 --> 00:14:04,980
A habit doesn't have to be perfect

373
00:14:04,980 --> 00:14:06,320
to be useful.

374
00:14:06,320 --> 00:14:09,270
Mathieu: You know what I rediscovered thanks to that?

375
00:14:09,320 --> 00:14:11,200
The taste of my coffee.

376
00:14:11,200 --> 00:14:12,430
Léa: Really?

377
00:14:12,480 --> 00:14:14,930
Mathieu: Yes! Before, I drank my coffee

378
00:14:14,930 --> 00:14:16,640
while looking at my screen.

379
00:14:16,640 --> 00:14:18,370
Now, sometimes,

380
00:14:18,370 --> 00:14:19,880
I sit by the window

381
00:14:19,880 --> 00:14:23,140
and I really take the time to savour it.

382
00:14:23,190 --> 00:14:25,240
Léa: That's a really lovely expression,

383
00:14:25,240 --> 00:14:27,920
"take the time to savour".

384
00:14:27,920 --> 00:14:31,240
Mathieu: Yes. Savouring isn't only about eating

385
00:14:31,240 --> 00:14:32,840
or drinking something.

386
00:14:32,840 --> 00:14:35,580
You can also savour a moment,

387
00:14:35,580 --> 00:14:39,480
a victory or a pleasant experience.

388
00:14:39,480 --> 00:14:42,290
Léa: For example, "After a long week,

389
00:14:42,290 --> 00:14:45,240
I savoured my quiet Sunday."

390
00:14:45,240 --> 00:14:46,190
Mathieu: Or "This morning,

391
00:14:46,190 --> 00:14:47,480
I took the time

392
00:14:47,480 --> 00:14:50,630
to savour my coffee without rushing."

393
00:14:50,680 --> 00:14:52,310
Léa: Exactly. And you know,

394
00:14:52,310 --> 00:14:54,550
I think it's these little moments

395
00:14:54,550 --> 00:14:57,880
that can make everyday life more pleasant.

396
00:14:57,880 --> 00:14:59,800
Mathieu: But Léa, let's imagine someone

397
00:14:59,800 --> 00:15:02,880
who really doesn't have much time in the morning.

398
00:15:02,880 --> 00:15:05,360
Someone who has to get their kids ready,

399
00:15:05,360 --> 00:15:07,110
take public transport,

400
00:15:07,110 --> 00:15:09,440
or start work very early.

401
00:15:09,440 --> 00:15:11,520
Léa: That's an excellent question.

402
00:15:11,520 --> 00:15:14,260
Because not everyone has the luxury

403
00:15:14,260 --> 00:15:16,840
of spending a quiet hour at home.

404
00:15:16,840 --> 00:15:20,110
Mathieu: Exactly. Sometimes, five minutes

405
00:15:20,110 --> 00:15:23,120
of silence already seem impossible.

406
00:15:23,120 --> 00:15:25,610
Léa: In that case, I think we need to fit

407
00:15:25,610 --> 00:15:27,610
small pleasant moments

408
00:15:27,610 --> 00:15:29,000
into what we already do.

409
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:30,280
Mathieu: For example?

410
00:15:30,280 --> 00:15:32,030
Léa: Listening to a song you love

411
00:15:32,030 --> 00:15:34,120
while you make breakfast.

412
00:15:34,120 --> 00:15:35,870
Breathing calmly

413
00:15:35,870 --> 00:15:37,840
while you wait for the water to heat up.

414
00:15:37,840 --> 00:15:40,890
Or taking a few seconds to look outside

415
00:15:40,890 --> 00:15:42,760
before leaving the house.

416
00:15:42,760 --> 00:15:44,700
Mathieu: So it's not necessarily about

417
00:15:44,700 --> 00:15:47,230
adding new activities.

418
00:15:47,280 --> 00:15:50,090
Léa: No. Sometimes it's simply about

419
00:15:50,090 --> 00:15:53,200
living the things we already do differently.

420
00:15:53,200 --> 00:15:55,040
Mathieu: I really like that idea.

421
00:15:55,040 --> 00:15:58,040
Because a routine that takes too much effort

422
00:15:58,040 --> 00:16:01,760
risks becoming one more obligation.

423
00:16:01,760 --> 00:16:04,320
Léa: Exactly. And if our routine

424
00:16:04,320 --> 00:16:05,960
stresses us out even more,

425
00:16:05,960 --> 00:16:08,440
it's no longer really doing its job.

426
00:16:08,440 --> 00:16:09,950
Mathieu: Speaking of stress,

427
00:16:09,950 --> 00:16:12,300
there's something I do often:

428
00:16:12,350 --> 00:16:13,770
as soon as I wake up,

429
00:16:13,770 --> 00:16:15,090
I start thinking

430
00:16:15,090 --> 00:16:17,480
about all my tasks for the day.

431
00:16:17,480 --> 00:16:18,040
Léa: Ah yes.

432
00:16:18,040 --> 00:16:20,700
The famous mental list that starts

433
00:16:20,700 --> 00:16:23,360
before we're even out of bed.

434
00:16:23,360 --> 00:16:26,320
Mathieu: Exactly. I think about an appointment,

435
00:16:26,320 --> 00:16:28,640
then about a document to finish,

436
00:16:28,640 --> 00:16:32,240
then about a message I haven't answered...

437
00:16:32,240 --> 00:16:33,850
Léa: And within a few seconds,

438
00:16:33,850 --> 00:16:34,980
you feel

439
00:16:34,980 --> 00:16:37,200
like your day is already too full.

440
00:16:37,200 --> 00:16:41,350
Mathieu: That's it. And yet, I haven't started anything.

441
00:16:41,400 --> 00:16:43,030
Léa: So maybe, instead

442
00:16:43,030 --> 00:16:44,480
of immediately thinking

443
00:16:44,480 --> 00:16:46,280
about everything you have to do,

444
00:16:46,280 --> 00:16:47,610
you could start

445
00:16:47,610 --> 00:16:49,520
with a simpler question.

446
00:16:49,520 --> 00:16:50,590
Mathieu: Which one?

447
00:16:50,640 --> 00:16:52,950
Léa: "What do I need today?"

448
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,390
Mathieu: Hmm... That's not the same thing

449
00:16:55,390 --> 00:16:57,870
as "What do I have to do today?"

450
00:16:57,920 --> 00:17:00,650
Léa: Exactly. The first question

451
00:17:00,650 --> 00:17:02,050
is about your state,

452
00:17:02,050 --> 00:17:04,320
your energy, your needs.

453
00:17:04,320 --> 00:17:07,240
The second one is about your obligations.

454
00:17:07,240 --> 00:17:09,390
Mathieu: And both are important.

455
00:17:09,440 --> 00:17:10,090
Léa: Of course.

456
00:17:10,090 --> 00:17:13,880
But sometimes we completely forget the first.

457
00:17:13,880 --> 00:17:16,120
Mathieu: So, if I feel tired,

458
00:17:16,120 --> 00:17:17,720
I could tell myself:

459
00:17:17,720 --> 00:17:20,760
today, I need to take it easy.

460
00:17:20,760 --> 00:17:24,050
Léa: Yes. Or if you have an important day:

461
00:17:24,050 --> 00:17:25,520
today, I need

462
00:17:25,520 --> 00:17:28,120
to stay focused on what matters.

463
00:17:28,120 --> 00:17:28,890
Mathieu: And if I know

464
00:17:28,890 --> 00:17:32,150
I'm going to meet a difficult person?

465
00:17:32,200 --> 00:17:36,200
Léa: Maybe: today, I need patience.

466
00:17:36,200 --> 00:17:38,980
Mathieu: A lot of patience, in some cases!

467
00:17:39,030 --> 00:17:41,190
Léa: Maybe! But you see,

468
00:17:41,190 --> 00:17:43,510
this little reflection can help us

469
00:17:43,510 --> 00:17:45,880
choose a direction for the day.

470
00:17:45,880 --> 00:17:47,820
Mathieu: Not a perfect plan.

471
00:17:47,820 --> 00:17:49,560
Just an intention.

472
00:17:49,560 --> 00:17:50,920
Léa: Exactly.

473
00:17:50,920 --> 00:17:53,720
And I really like the word "intention".

474
00:17:53,720 --> 00:17:55,690
An intention is something

475
00:17:55,690 --> 00:17:58,120
you want to do, or a way

476
00:17:58,120 --> 00:17:59,480
you want to act.

477
00:17:59,480 --> 00:18:01,700
Mathieu: For example: "Today,

478
00:18:01,700 --> 00:18:04,150
my intention is to take things

479
00:18:04,150 --> 00:18:05,700
more calmly."

480
00:18:05,750 --> 00:18:06,610
Léa: Very good. Or:

481
00:18:06,610 --> 00:18:08,840
"This week, I intend

482
00:18:08,840 --> 00:18:11,000
to organise my time better."

483
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:12,750
Mathieu: But I have a question.

484
00:18:12,750 --> 00:18:15,590
What if we don't stick to our intention?

485
00:18:15,640 --> 00:18:17,960
Léa: Well, it's not the end of the world.

486
00:18:17,960 --> 00:18:19,850
Mathieu: Even if I wanted to stay calm

487
00:18:19,850 --> 00:18:22,150
and I got annoyed before noon?

488
00:18:22,200 --> 00:18:24,280
Léa: Even then.

489
00:18:24,280 --> 00:18:27,050
You can acknowledge what happened

490
00:18:27,050 --> 00:18:30,200
and try to do things differently afterwards.

491
00:18:30,200 --> 00:18:31,480
Mathieu: So we don't need

492
00:18:31,480 --> 00:18:33,340
to give up on the whole day

493
00:18:33,340 --> 00:18:35,310
after one difficult moment.

494
00:18:35,360 --> 00:18:38,680
Léa: Exactly. Because a complicated morning

495
00:18:38,680 --> 00:18:41,640
doesn't necessarily mean a bad day.

496
00:18:41,640 --> 00:18:44,440
Mathieu: That reminds me of last Tuesday.

497
00:18:44,440 --> 00:18:45,410
I'd decided

498
00:18:45,410 --> 00:18:48,040
to start my day calmly.

499
00:18:48,040 --> 00:18:50,120
And then, one thing led to another.

500
00:18:50,120 --> 00:18:51,430
Léa: What happened?

501
00:18:51,480 --> 00:18:54,600
Mathieu: First, I spilled my coffee.

502
00:18:54,600 --> 00:18:57,640
Then, I couldn't find my keys.

503
00:18:57,640 --> 00:18:59,960
And when I finally went out,

504
00:18:59,960 --> 00:19:01,880
it started to rain.

505
00:19:01,880 --> 00:19:04,670
Léa: Oh dear! What a morning!

506
00:19:04,720 --> 00:19:08,910
Mathieu: Yes. And straight away I told myself:

507
00:19:08,910 --> 00:19:11,870
that's it, my whole day is ruined.

508
00:19:11,920 --> 00:19:13,990
Léa: "My day is ruined" —

509
00:19:14,040 --> 00:19:16,520
it's a very informal expression.

510
00:19:16,520 --> 00:19:17,950
It means you think

511
00:19:17,950 --> 00:19:20,360
the day is completely spoilt.

512
00:19:20,360 --> 00:19:23,800
Mathieu: Exactly. But in reality,

513
00:19:23,800 --> 00:19:26,120
it was only quarter past eight.

514
00:19:26,120 --> 00:19:28,520
Léa: So you still had plenty of time

515
00:19:28,520 --> 00:19:30,440
to live better moments.

516
00:19:30,440 --> 00:19:32,900
Mathieu: Yes. And in the end,

517
00:19:32,900 --> 00:19:35,640
the afternoon went really well.

518
00:19:35,640 --> 00:19:37,800
I finished an important project,

519
00:19:37,800 --> 00:19:40,840
and I even met up with a friend for lunch.

520
00:19:40,840 --> 00:19:41,510
Léa: You see!

521
00:19:41,510 --> 00:19:44,260
Your bad morning hadn't decided

522
00:19:44,260 --> 00:19:46,120
the rest of your day.

523
00:19:46,120 --> 00:19:49,400
Mathieu: True. But in the moment,

524
00:19:49,400 --> 00:19:51,800
I found it hard to step back.

525
00:19:51,800 --> 00:19:54,840
Léa: That's another interesting expression:

526
00:19:54,840 --> 00:19:56,910
"prendre du recul" (to step back).

527
00:19:56,960 --> 00:19:59,720
It means looking at a situation

528
00:19:59,720 --> 00:20:01,640
with a bit of distance,

529
00:20:01,640 --> 00:20:03,390
without letting yourself be completely

530
00:20:03,390 --> 00:20:05,000
carried away by your emotions.

531
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:08,040
Mathieu: For example: "When I'm stressed,

532
00:20:08,040 --> 00:20:11,320
I try to step back before reacting."

533
00:20:11,320 --> 00:20:14,770
Léa: Exactly. And sometimes, you just need to remember

534
00:20:14,770 --> 00:20:16,040
that a bad hour

535
00:20:16,040 --> 00:20:18,120
isn't a bad day.

536
00:20:18,120 --> 00:20:19,960
Mathieu: I like that idea.

537
00:20:19,960 --> 00:20:21,710
You can always find

538
00:20:21,710 --> 00:20:23,470
a bit of calm again later.

539
00:20:23,520 --> 00:20:28,200
Léa: Yes. At noon, in the afternoon, or even in the evening.

540
00:20:28,200 --> 00:20:30,360
You don't have to wait until the next day

541
00:20:30,360 --> 00:20:32,600
to start feeling better again.

542
00:20:32,600 --> 00:20:34,850
Mathieu: You know, that makes me think

543
00:20:34,850 --> 00:20:37,960
of another habit I'd like to adopt:

544
00:20:37,960 --> 00:20:41,240
doing one single small thing first thing in the morning,

545
00:20:41,240 --> 00:20:43,240
and finishing it completely.

546
00:20:43,240 --> 00:20:45,030
Léa: Oh yes? Like what?

547
00:20:45,080 --> 00:20:46,110
Mathieu: For example,

548
00:20:46,110 --> 00:20:49,230
putting my cup away after breakfast,

549
00:20:49,280 --> 00:20:50,520
getting my bag ready,

550
00:20:50,520 --> 00:20:53,800
or putting something back in its place.

551
00:20:53,800 --> 00:20:55,550
Léa: That's a good idea.

552
00:20:55,550 --> 00:20:57,160
Because a small finished task

553
00:20:57,160 --> 00:20:59,960
can give a feeling of satisfaction.

554
00:20:59,960 --> 00:21:02,140
Mathieu: Yes. I've noticed

555
00:21:02,140 --> 00:21:04,920
that when I finish one simple thing,

556
00:21:04,920 --> 00:21:06,670
I sometimes feel more ready

557
00:21:06,670 --> 00:21:08,440
to start the next one.

558
00:21:08,440 --> 00:21:10,700
Léa: In French, we could say:

559
00:21:10,750 --> 00:21:13,190
"It gets me into a good momentum."

560
00:21:13,240 --> 00:21:15,560
Mathieu: A good momentum.

561
00:21:15,560 --> 00:21:17,550
I like that expression.

562
00:21:17,600 --> 00:21:19,230
Léa: It means you get into

563
00:21:19,230 --> 00:21:21,080
a positive movement,

564
00:21:21,080 --> 00:21:23,720
a rhythm that helps you move forward.

565
00:21:23,720 --> 00:21:25,000
Mathieu: For example:

566
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:26,200
"Starting the day

567
00:21:26,200 --> 00:21:28,210
with a little walk gets me

568
00:21:28,210 --> 00:21:30,040
into a good momentum."

569
00:21:30,040 --> 00:21:33,240
Léa: Or: "When my desk is tidy, I feel

570
00:21:33,240 --> 00:21:35,800
in a better groove for working."

571
00:21:35,800 --> 00:21:37,350
Mathieu: But careful,

572
00:21:37,400 --> 00:21:38,960
I don't want people to think

573
00:21:38,960 --> 00:21:41,230
they have to clean their whole house

574
00:21:41,230 --> 00:21:42,520
before leaving!

575
00:21:42,520 --> 00:21:44,360
Léa: No, of course not!

576
00:21:44,360 --> 00:21:47,140
One single small action can be enough.

577
00:21:47,190 --> 00:21:48,760
Mathieu: That's reassuring.

578
00:21:48,760 --> 00:21:50,480
Because if I start

579
00:21:50,480 --> 00:21:52,960
tidying my flat, I risk

580
00:21:52,960 --> 00:21:55,880
discovering fifteen other things to do.

581
00:21:55,880 --> 00:21:57,760
Léa: And then, your morning routine

582
00:21:57,760 --> 00:22:00,840
becomes a whole day of housework!

583
00:22:00,840 --> 00:22:04,360
Mathieu: Exactly. That wasn't the plan.

584
00:22:04,360 --> 00:22:05,790
Léa: You know, Mathieu,

585
00:22:05,790 --> 00:22:07,750
I'd like to talk about something

586
00:22:07,750 --> 00:22:09,000
a little different.

587
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,480
For me, a pleasant morning

588
00:22:11,480 --> 00:22:12,600
isn't only

589
00:22:12,600 --> 00:22:14,280
a question of organisation.

590
00:22:14,280 --> 00:22:17,060
Mathieu: Oh yes? What do you mean?

591
00:22:17,110 --> 00:22:18,750
Léa: I think it's important

592
00:22:18,750 --> 00:22:21,400
to keep a little room for pleasure.

593
00:22:21,400 --> 00:22:24,840
Mathieu: Now that's a topic I fully approve of.

594
00:22:24,840 --> 00:22:27,800
Léa: I'm not necessarily talking about big things.

595
00:22:27,800 --> 00:22:30,840
For example, listening to your favourite music,

596
00:22:30,840 --> 00:22:33,320
making a breakfast you love,

597
00:22:33,320 --> 00:22:35,560
or reading a few pages of a book.

598
00:22:35,560 --> 00:22:38,440
Mathieu: Or eating a croissant that's still warm.

599
00:22:38,440 --> 00:22:39,210
Léa: Of course!

600
00:22:39,210 --> 00:22:42,360
I knew you were going to talk about food.

601
00:22:42,360 --> 00:22:44,670
Mathieu: A good croissant can improve

602
00:22:44,670 --> 00:22:46,580
so many things.

603
00:22:46,630 --> 00:22:48,440
Léa: You know, when I was little,

604
00:22:48,440 --> 00:22:50,350
my mother often put music on

605
00:22:50,350 --> 00:22:53,000
in the kitchen on Sunday mornings.

606
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:55,480
Mathieu: Ah, that's a lovely memory.

607
00:22:55,480 --> 00:22:58,360
Léa: Yes. She made breakfast,

608
00:22:58,360 --> 00:23:00,680
the light came into the room,

609
00:23:00,680 --> 00:23:03,800
and sometimes, we sang together.

610
00:23:03,800 --> 00:23:06,200
Not always very much in tune, by the way.

611
00:23:06,200 --> 00:23:08,200
Mathieu: But that didn't matter.

612
00:23:08,200 --> 00:23:10,490
Léa: Exactly. What mattered

613
00:23:10,490 --> 00:23:12,420
was that atmosphere.

614
00:23:12,470 --> 00:23:15,360
It felt like the day was starting

615
00:23:15,360 --> 00:23:16,760
with a little joy.

616
00:23:16,760 --> 00:23:18,030
Mathieu: You see, I think

617
00:23:18,030 --> 00:23:19,800
the warmest memories

618
00:23:19,800 --> 00:23:21,160
don't always come

619
00:23:21,160 --> 00:23:23,160
from extraordinary things.

620
00:23:23,160 --> 00:23:25,900
Léa: Yes. Often, it's small details

621
00:23:25,900 --> 00:23:28,520
that we end up associating with happiness.

622
00:23:28,520 --> 00:23:30,360
Mathieu: The smell of toast,

623
00:23:30,360 --> 00:23:33,880
the sound of rain, a song you love...

624
00:23:33,880 --> 00:23:36,290
Léa: Or simply a few minutes spent

625
00:23:36,290 --> 00:23:38,120
with someone you love.

626
00:23:38,120 --> 00:23:39,060
Mathieu: That reminds me

627
00:23:39,060 --> 00:23:41,310
of mornings at my grandparents' house.

628
00:23:41,360 --> 00:23:43,280
My grandfather always got up

629
00:23:43,280 --> 00:23:44,680
before everyone else.

630
00:23:44,680 --> 00:23:46,070
Léa: What did he do?

631
00:23:46,120 --> 00:23:47,870
Mathieu: He made coffee,

632
00:23:47,870 --> 00:23:50,570
then he sat by the window

633
00:23:50,570 --> 00:23:51,720
with his newspaper.

634
00:23:51,720 --> 00:23:53,470
He always looked calm,

635
00:23:53,470 --> 00:23:56,200
even when he had lots to do.

636
00:23:56,200 --> 00:23:59,160
Léa: Maybe he'd found his own rhythm.

637
00:23:59,160 --> 00:24:01,790
Mathieu: Yes. And when I asked him

638
00:24:01,790 --> 00:24:03,720
why he didn't hurry,

639
00:24:03,720 --> 00:24:05,150
he'd answer:

640
00:24:05,200 --> 00:24:07,130
"The day will be long enough,

641
00:24:07,130 --> 00:24:09,620
no point starting it at a run."

642
00:24:09,670 --> 00:24:12,280
Léa: I love that sentence.

643
00:24:12,280 --> 00:24:13,560
Mathieu: Me too.

644
00:24:13,560 --> 00:24:15,310
I didn't always understand it

645
00:24:15,310 --> 00:24:16,590
when I was younger,

646
00:24:16,590 --> 00:24:19,320
but today, it really speaks to me.

647
00:24:19,320 --> 00:24:21,070
Léa: Because we sometimes live

648
00:24:21,070 --> 00:24:23,640
as if every minute had to be filled.

649
00:24:23,640 --> 00:24:27,320
Mathieu: Exactly. Even during our moments of rest,

650
00:24:27,320 --> 00:24:28,440
we sometimes wonder

651
00:24:28,440 --> 00:24:29,880
whether we could be doing something

652
00:24:29,880 --> 00:24:31,480
more useful.

653
00:24:31,480 --> 00:24:33,840
Léa: Whereas we also have the right to enjoy

654
00:24:33,840 --> 00:24:37,230
a moment simply because it's pleasant.

655
00:24:37,280 --> 00:24:40,840
Mathieu: Without needing to turn it into a goal.

656
00:24:40,840 --> 00:24:41,960
Léa: Absolutely.

657
00:24:41,960 --> 00:24:44,680
And that can start first thing in the morning.

658
00:24:44,680 --> 00:24:46,430
Mathieu: But I'd like to come back

659
00:24:46,430 --> 00:24:48,120
to one important thing.

660
00:24:48,120 --> 00:24:50,860
We talk a lot about good habits,

661
00:24:50,860 --> 00:24:54,280
but sometimes, we're simply exhausted.

662
00:24:54,280 --> 00:24:57,240
Léa: Yes. And it's important to admit it.

663
00:24:57,240 --> 00:25:00,440
Mathieu: For example, if someone works very late,

664
00:25:00,440 --> 00:25:02,740
looks after a baby during the night

665
00:25:02,790 --> 00:25:04,990
or is going through a difficult time,

666
00:25:04,990 --> 00:25:06,320
a perfect routine

667
00:25:06,320 --> 00:25:08,920
won't solve all their problems.

668
00:25:08,920 --> 00:25:12,430
Léa: Absolutely. We mustn't turn tiredness

669
00:25:12,430 --> 00:25:14,040
into a lack of willpower.

670
00:25:14,040 --> 00:25:16,780
Mathieu: Because sometimes, we need rest,

671
00:25:16,780 --> 00:25:19,560
not a new to-do list.

672
00:25:19,560 --> 00:25:22,300
Léa: And a good routine should be adapted

673
00:25:22,300 --> 00:25:23,720
to our reality.

674
00:25:23,720 --> 00:25:25,160
Not to an ideal life

675
00:25:25,160 --> 00:25:27,160
that we see on social media.

676
00:25:27,160 --> 00:25:29,070
Mathieu: That's very important.

677
00:25:29,120 --> 00:25:30,400
Because I've already tried

678
00:25:30,400 --> 00:25:33,320
to copy someone's routine from the internet.

679
00:25:33,320 --> 00:25:35,320
Léa: Oh really? Tell me.

680
00:25:35,320 --> 00:25:38,230
Mathieu: He got up at five, did exercise,

681
00:25:38,230 --> 00:25:41,480
read thirty pages, wrote in a notebook,

682
00:25:41,480 --> 00:25:44,840
and made a magnificent breakfast.

683
00:25:44,840 --> 00:25:45,830
Léa: And you?

684
00:25:45,880 --> 00:25:49,000
Mathieu: The first day, I was very motivated.

685
00:25:49,000 --> 00:25:51,240
The second, a bit less.

686
00:25:51,240 --> 00:25:53,720
The third, I turned off my alarm

687
00:25:53,720 --> 00:25:55,480
and went back to sleep.

688
00:25:55,480 --> 00:25:57,140
Léa: Oh, Mathieu!

689
00:25:57,190 --> 00:25:58,840
Mathieu: And for a while,

690
00:25:58,840 --> 00:26:01,640
I thought I lacked discipline.

691
00:26:01,640 --> 00:26:02,990
Léa: Whereas in reality,

692
00:26:02,990 --> 00:26:05,560
that routine maybe just

693
00:26:05,560 --> 00:26:07,800
didn't match your rhythm.

694
00:26:07,800 --> 00:26:10,830
Mathieu: Exactly. I wanted to adopt the life

695
00:26:10,830 --> 00:26:12,410
of someone else,

696
00:26:12,410 --> 00:26:14,920
instead of understanding what suited me.

697
00:26:14,920 --> 00:26:17,240
Léa: That's a lovely realisation.

698
00:26:17,240 --> 00:26:18,910
Mathieu: Yes. Today,

699
00:26:18,910 --> 00:26:20,360
I prefer having a routine

700
00:26:20,360 --> 00:26:21,720
that's much simpler,

701
00:26:21,720 --> 00:26:24,440
but that I can actually follow.

702
00:26:24,440 --> 00:26:27,070
Léa: And I think that's a good approach.

703
00:26:27,120 --> 00:26:30,200
A realistic habit is often easier

704
00:26:30,200 --> 00:26:31,080
to keep.

705
00:26:31,080 --> 00:26:33,400
Mathieu: Even if it's not very impressive.

706
00:26:33,400 --> 00:26:36,760
Léa: Especially if it's not impressive!

707
00:26:36,760 --> 00:26:38,510
Mathieu: So, if I understand correctly,

708
00:26:38,510 --> 00:26:40,360
a good morning routine

709
00:26:40,360 --> 00:26:43,000
isn't a list of ten rules.

710
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:44,740
Léa: No. It's more like

711
00:26:44,740 --> 00:26:47,400
a small collection of habits

712
00:26:47,400 --> 00:26:49,880
that help us feel better.

713
00:26:49,880 --> 00:26:52,350
Mathieu: And that can change depending on the periods

714
00:26:52,350 --> 00:26:53,320
of our life.

715
00:26:53,320 --> 00:26:55,840
Léa: Exactly. There are periods

716
00:26:55,840 --> 00:26:57,640
when we have lots of time.

717
00:26:57,640 --> 00:27:00,200
Others when we have very little.

718
00:27:00,200 --> 00:27:02,730
Mathieu: And sometimes, what did us good

719
00:27:02,730 --> 00:27:05,670
two years ago doesn't match at all

720
00:27:05,670 --> 00:27:07,560
what we need today.

721
00:27:07,560 --> 00:27:08,680
Léa: That's true.

722
00:27:08,680 --> 00:27:11,150
So we have to learn to listen to ourselves

723
00:27:11,150 --> 00:27:13,400
and adjust our habits.

724
00:27:13,400 --> 00:27:14,670
Mathieu: You know, I think

725
00:27:14,670 --> 00:27:17,400
I've just understood something.

726
00:27:17,400 --> 00:27:20,360
I thought a good routine was a routine

727
00:27:20,360 --> 00:27:22,360
you followed perfectly.

728
00:27:22,360 --> 00:27:23,630
Léa: And now?

729
00:27:23,680 --> 00:27:26,440
Mathieu: Now, I think a good routine

730
00:27:26,440 --> 00:27:27,840
is a routine

731
00:27:27,840 --> 00:27:30,680
you want to come back to.

732
00:27:30,680 --> 00:27:33,640
Léa: I absolutely love that definition.

733
00:27:33,640 --> 00:27:36,760
Mathieu: Thanks. Maybe I'll write it down somewhere.

734
00:27:36,760 --> 00:27:38,550
Léa: Why not on your alarm clock?

735
00:27:38,600 --> 00:27:40,760
Mathieu: No, definitely not.

736
00:27:40,760 --> 00:27:43,640
I'd rather stay on good terms with it.

737
00:27:43,640 --> 00:27:46,840
Léa: So, Mathieu, let's imagine tomorrow morning.

738
00:27:46,840 --> 00:27:49,720
You wake up, you have a bit of time,

739
00:27:49,720 --> 00:27:50,830
and you want to start

740
00:27:50,830 --> 00:27:53,300
your day more pleasantly.

741
00:27:53,350 --> 00:27:54,630
What do you do?

742
00:27:54,680 --> 00:27:56,760
Mathieu: First, I resist the urge

743
00:27:56,760 --> 00:27:59,880
to look at my phone straight away.

744
00:27:59,880 --> 00:28:01,830
Léa: Very good. Then?

745
00:28:01,880 --> 00:28:05,080
Mathieu: I open the curtains, I drink a glass of water,

746
00:28:05,080 --> 00:28:06,730
and I take a few moments

747
00:28:06,730 --> 00:28:08,840
to wake up calmly.

748
00:28:08,840 --> 00:28:09,830
Léa: And then?

749
00:28:09,880 --> 00:28:12,760
Mathieu: I make my coffee, obviously.

750
00:28:12,760 --> 00:28:14,040
Léa: Obviously!

751
00:28:14,040 --> 00:28:17,180
Mathieu: But this time, I take the time to drink it

752
00:28:17,180 --> 00:28:20,440
without doing three other things at once.

753
00:28:20,440 --> 00:28:21,780
Léa: Very good idea.

754
00:28:21,830 --> 00:28:23,090
Mathieu: And if I have time,

755
00:28:23,090 --> 00:28:25,160
I go for a little walk.

756
00:28:25,160 --> 00:28:27,970
Otherwise, I can simply listen to a song

757
00:28:27,970 --> 00:28:30,840
I love before I start working.

758
00:28:30,840 --> 00:28:33,240
Léa: You see, nothing complicated.

759
00:28:33,240 --> 00:28:34,990
Mathieu: Yes. And above all,

760
00:28:34,990 --> 00:28:35,980
I don't need

761
00:28:35,980 --> 00:28:37,800
to feel guilty if tomorrow

762
00:28:37,800 --> 00:28:40,840
doesn't go exactly as planned.

763
00:28:40,840 --> 00:28:43,910
Léa: That's it. Because the goal isn't to have

764
00:28:43,910 --> 00:28:45,720
perfect mornings.

765
00:28:45,720 --> 00:28:47,070
Mathieu: The goal is to make

766
00:28:47,070 --> 00:28:49,700
our mornings a little more pleasant,

767
00:28:49,700 --> 00:28:51,070
little by little.

768
00:28:51,120 --> 00:28:53,140
Léa: Exactly. And maybe

769
00:28:53,140 --> 00:28:54,920
a gentler routine

770
00:28:54,920 --> 00:28:56,600
can also change the way

771
00:28:56,600 --> 00:28:59,400
we approach the rest of the day.

772
00:28:59,400 --> 00:29:01,750
Mathieu: Not because it makes us invincible.

773
00:29:01,800 --> 00:29:03,400
Léa: No, of course not.

774
00:29:03,400 --> 00:29:05,840
Mathieu: But because it sometimes gives us

775
00:29:05,840 --> 00:29:08,280
a little more space to breathe,

776
00:29:08,280 --> 00:29:12,040
think and choose how to move forward.

777
00:29:12,040 --> 00:29:14,280
Léa: That's very beautifully put, Mathieu.

778
00:29:14,280 --> 00:29:16,030
Mathieu: Thanks. I think

779
00:29:16,030 --> 00:29:18,760
my coffee is starting to kick in.

780
00:29:18,760 --> 00:29:20,590
Léa: Ah, now I understand!

781
00:29:20,640 --> 00:29:22,340
Mathieu: Okay, Léa, we've used

782
00:29:22,340 --> 00:29:25,640
several interesting expressions today.

783
00:29:25,640 --> 00:29:27,390
I think it's the right time

784
00:29:27,390 --> 00:29:29,400
to take a closer look at them.

785
00:29:29,400 --> 00:29:30,920
Léa: Great idea.

786
00:29:30,920 --> 00:29:33,880
Let's start with a very useful expression:

787
00:29:33,880 --> 00:29:35,400
"prendre son temps" (to take your time).

788
00:29:35,400 --> 00:29:37,470
Mathieu: Taking your time means

789
00:29:37,470 --> 00:29:40,260
doing something calmly,

790
00:29:40,260 --> 00:29:41,800
without rushing.

791
00:29:41,800 --> 00:29:44,510
Léa: For example: "On Sunday mornings,

792
00:29:44,510 --> 00:29:46,290
I like to take my time

793
00:29:46,290 --> 00:29:48,340
to make breakfast."

794
00:29:48,390 --> 00:29:51,640
Mathieu: Or: "Don't rush, take your time."

795
00:29:51,640 --> 00:29:54,200
Léa: Exactly. And careful,

796
00:29:54,200 --> 00:29:55,480
taking your time

797
00:29:55,480 --> 00:29:58,330
doesn't necessarily mean being slow

798
00:29:58,330 --> 00:30:00,720
or lazy.
